{"name":"Wild Elderberry","hex":"#545989","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wild","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Untamed; not domesticated.","Especially, being of the wild type: being of an unbroken ancestral line of undomesticated animals, as opposed to being feral, being an undomesticated animal whose ancestors were domesticated."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Inaccurately; not on target.","Intended to be synchronized with film or video but recorded separately."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wild"},{"term":"elderberry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any shrub or tree of the genus Sambucus;","The small, edible, purplish-black fruit of this plant, used in cooking and to flavor drinks, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/elderberry"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sambucus","blocks":[{"text":"Sambucus is a genus of between 20 and 30 species of flowering plants in the family Viburnaceae. The various species are commonly referred to as elder, with the flowers as elderflower, and the fruit as elderberry. The genus is represented by species native to temperate and subtropical climates in most parts of the world, including Eurasia, the Americas, Africa and Australia, and has been introduced to other regions of similar climates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Sambucus-berries.jpg/330px-Sambucus-berries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sambucus","wikidata":"Q131448"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Sambucus_canadensis3.jpg/500px-Sambucus_canadensis3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f6/Sambucus_canadensis3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikip